  </div>

  <div class="chart-container">
    <img src="data:image/png;base64,{hist_b64}" alt="Histograms of price distribution by fuel type">
  </div>

  <div class="insight-box">
    <strong>Key Observation:</strong> {most_expensive} is the most expensive fuel type with a median price of {most_exp_median:.1f}p per litre, while {least_expensive} is the least expensive at {least_exp_median:.1f}p. The median difference between the most and least expensive fuel types is <strong>{most_exp_median - least_exp_median:.1f}p</strong> per litre (£{(most_exp_median - least_exp_median)/100:.2f}).
  </div>
</section>

<!-- Section 3: Price Spread & Market Competitiveness -->
<section id="price-spread-analysis">
  <h2>3. Price Spread &amp; Market Competitiveness</h2>
  <p>The price spread — measured by both the full range (max minus min) and the interquartile range (IQR) — indicates how much price variation consumers encounter. A wider spread suggests greater price disparity and potentially less competitive local markets.</p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Fuel Type</th><th>Min</th><th>Q1</th><th>Median</th><th>Q3</th><th>Max</th><th>IQR</th><th>Full Range</th><th>Range (£/L)</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {spread_table_rows()}
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="chart-container">
    <img src="data:image/png;base64,{spread_b64}" alt="Price spread comparison by fuel type">
  </div>

  <div class="insight-box">
    <strong>Competitiveness Assessment:</strong> {widest_range_ft} exhibits the widest full price range at <strong>{widest_range_val:.1f}p</strong> (£{widest_range_val/100:.2f} per litre), suggesting the greatest variation across the market. However, the IQR provides a more robust measure of typical consumer experience. {narrowest_iqr_ft} has the tightest IQR at <strong>{narrowest_iqr_val:.1f}p</strong>, indicating the most competitive pricing within the core market.
  </div>

  <div class="warning-box">
    <strong>Policy Consideration:</strong> Large full-range spreads may be driven by outlier stations (e.g., motorway services or remote locations) rather than systemic price unfairness. The IQR is a better indicator of the price variation that typical consumers face. Where the IQR is wide, this suggests meaningful within-market price disparity warranting further investigation.
  </div>
</section>

<!-- Section 4: Update Freshness Analysis -->
<section id="update-freshness">
  <h2>4. Retailer Reporting Freshness Analysis</h2>
  <p>The update lag measures the time difference between when a retailer last updated their price (<code>source_updated_at</code>) and when the data collection system recorded it (<code>recorded_at</code>). Shorter lags indicate more timely and transparent reporting.</p>

  <h3>Overall Lag Statistics</h3>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Metric</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Mean Lag</td><td>{lag_stats['mean']:.1f} hours</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Median Lag</td><td>{lag_stats['median']:.1f} hours</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Standard Deviation</td><td>{lag_stats['std']:.1f} hours</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Minimum Lag</td><td>{lag_stats['min']:.1f} hours</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Maximum Lag</td><td>{lag_stats['max']:.1f} hours</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Records within 1 hour</td><td>{lag_stats['pct_under_1h']:.1f}%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Records within 6 hours</td><td>{lag_stats['pct_under_6h']:.1f}%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Records within 24 hours</td><td>{lag_stats['pct_under_24h']:.1f}%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Records with lag &gt; 48 hours</td><td>{lag_stats['pct_over_48h']:.1f}%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Records with lag &gt; 7 days</td><td>{lag_stats['pct_over_168h']:.1f}%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Negative lag (source ahead of record)</td><td>{lag_stats['negative_count']:,} ({lag_stats['negative_pct']:.2f}%)</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="chart-container">
    <img src="data:image/png;base64,{lag_hist_b64}" alt="Distribution of update lag">
  </div>

  <h3>Update Lag by Fuel Type</h3>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Fuel Type</th><th>Median Lag (h)</th><th>Mean Lag (h)</th><th>Std Dev (h)</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {lag_fuel_table_rows()}
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="chart-container">
    <img src="data:image/png;base64,{lag_fuel_b64}" alt="Median update lag by fuel type">
  </div>

  {"<div class='warning-box'><strong>Data Quality Flag:</strong> " + str(lag_stats['negative_count']) + " records (" + f"{lag_stats['negative_pct']:.2f}" + "%) have a negative lag, meaning <code>source_updated_at</code> is later than <code>recorded_at</code>. This likely reflects clock synchronisation issues or batch-processing artefacts rather than actual future-dated updates.</div>" if lag_stats['negative_count'] > 0 else ""}

  {"<div class='warning-box'><strong>Stale Data Concern:</strong> " + str(n_stale) + " stations (" + f"{n_stale/unique_stations*100:.1f}" + "% of all stations) have at least one record where the price update lag exceeds 7 days. This raises questions about whether these retailers are actively maintaining their reported prices, which is critical for consumer trust in the open data scheme.</div>" if n_stale > 0 else ""}
</section>

<!-- Section 5: Station Coverage & Fuel Type Availability -->
<section id="station-coverage">
  <h2>5. Station Coverage &amp; Fuel Type Availability</h2>
  <p>Not all stations report prices for every fuel type. This section examines how comprehensively stations participate in the reporting scheme and which fuel types enjoy the broadest coverage.</p>

  <h3>Station Coverage by Fuel Type</h3>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Fuel Type</th><th>Unique Stations</th><th>% of All Stations</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {coverage_table_rows()}
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>Fuel Types Reported per Station</h3>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Number of Fuel Types</th><th>Number of Stations</th><th>% of Stations</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {station_fuel_dist_rows()}
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="chart-container">
    <img src="data:image/png;base64,{coverage_b64}" alt="Station coverage analysis">
  </div>

  <h3>Records per Station</h3>
  <p>On average, each station contributes <strong>{rps_stats['mean']:.1f}</strong> records (median: {rps_stats['median']:.0f}). The range spans from {rps_stats['min']} to {rps_stats['max']:,} records per station, reflecting variation in update frequency and reporting duration across the dataset's time window.</p>

  <div class="insight-box">
    <strong>Coverage Insight:</strong> The most widely reported fuel types provide the most robust basis for price analysis. Fuel types with narrow station coverage should be interpreted with caution, as their statistics may not be representative of the broader market.
  </div>
</section>

<!-- Section 6: Key Findings & Policy Implications -->
<section id="key-findings">
  <h2>6. Key Findings &amp; Policy Implications</h2>
  <p>The following findings synthesise the analytical results into actionable insights for fuel pricing policy, consumer protection, and market transparency assessment.</p>

  <ol class="findings-list">
    <li>
      <strong>Finding 1: Substantial price variation exists across all fuel types.</strong><br>
      {most_expensive} commands the highest median price at {most_exp_median:.1f}p/litre, while {least_expensive} is the most affordable at {least_exp_median:.1f}p/litre — a gap of {most_exp_median - least_exp_median:.1f}p. Within individual fuel types, full price ranges of up to {widest_range_val:.0f}p ({widest_range_ft}) indicate extreme outliers in the market.
      <div class="policy"><strong>Policy Implication:</strong> The wide price ranges suggest that some consumers may face significantly higher costs depending on their choice of forecourt. Enhanced price comparison tools and consumer awareness initiatives could help mitigate this disparity.</div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Finding 2: Core market pricing is more competitive than headline ranges suggest.</strong><br>
      The IQR for most fuel types is considerably narrower than the full range, indicating that the majority of stations price within a relatively tight band. {narrowest_iqr_ft} shows the tightest core pricing with an IQR of just {narrowest_iqr_val:.1f}p.
      <div class="policy"><strong>Policy Implication:</strong> While outlier pricing warrants scrutiny, the competitive core market suggests that the open data scheme is supporting price transparency for most consumers. Regulatory focus may be best directed at understanding why outlier stations deviate significantly.</div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Finding 3: The median update lag is {lag_stats['median']:.1f} hours, but a tail of stale data persists.</strong><br>
      {lag_stats['pct_under_1h']:.1f}% of records have a lag under 1 hour, and {lag_stats['pct_under_24h']:.1f}% are within 24 hours. However, {lag_stats['pct_over_48h']:.1f}% of records show lags exceeding 48 hours, and {n_stale} stations have data stale by more than 7 days.
      <div class="policy"><strong>Policy Implication:</strong> Most retailers report promptly, but the long tail of stale data undermines the reliability of the open data scheme. A compliance threshold (e.g., mandatory updates within 24 hours of any price change) could improve data freshness and consumer trust.</div>
