    <li>
      <strong>Finding 4: Station coverage varies significantly by fuel type.</strong><br>
      {fuel_station_coverage.index[0]} has the broadest station coverage ({fuel_station_coverage.iloc[0]:,} stations), while {fuel_station_coverage.index[-1]} has the narrowest ({fuel_station_coverage.iloc[-1]:,} stations). This reflects genuine market availability differences — not all stations sell premium or specialty fuels.
      <div class="policy"><strong>Policy Implication:</strong> Price statistics for fuel types with narrow station coverage should be interpreted with caution in policy analysis. The reporting mandate should ensure that all fuel types sold at a forecourt are reported, not just the most common ones.</div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Finding 5: The dataset of {total_records:,} records across {unique_stations:,} stations provides a robust basis for market monitoring.</strong><br>
      With complete data (no null values), multiple fuel types, and both standard and premium grades represented, the UK open data scheme is generating a valuable resource for market surveillance.
      <div class="policy"><strong>Policy Implication:</strong> This dataset supports ongoing programmatic monitoring of fuel market competitiveness. Regular automated reporting — extending this analysis with time-series trend detection and anomaly flagging — could form the basis of a proactive regulatory dashboard.</div>
